---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/apps/attentive';
const title = 'Attentive for Shopify: acquisition, service scope, and a practical trial';
const desc = 'Evaluate Attentive capture, messaging operations, service responsibilities, and quoted costs against your existing Shopify stack.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <a href="/apps">All app reviews</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Acquisition and operating support</p>
    <h1>Attentive for Shopify: evaluate the capture journey and the team behind it</h1>
    <p>Consider Attentive when subscriber acquisition or ongoing campaign operations is the bottleneck you need to resolve. The buying question is whether its capture journey, messaging tools, and included support reduce work your team actually performs. This is a proposed evaluation, not a hands-on performance test.</p>
    <h2>What Attentive advertises</h2>
    <p>Checked November 25, 2026: the <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/attentive">developer's Shopify listing</a> describes email and SMS, two-tap signup units, segmentation, and strategy support. These are advertised capabilities; the listing does not prove a conversion improvement for your storefront.</p>
    <p>The <a href="https://www.attentive.com/pricing">official pricing page</a> describes a modular proposal based on messages, subscribers, channels, and selected AI tools. It lists onboarding and strategic support among plan inclusions. Ask for the actual deliverables and service boundaries in the proposal: advice, campaign production, and approval responsibility are different jobs.</p>
    <h2>Start with a capture-to-message demonstration</h2>
    <p>Use your mobile storefront and one real acquisition promise, such as access to a product release. Have the demonstrator show the complete path from signup to the first message. Record the disclosure presented, the fields saved, how an existing subscriber is handled, and how a shopper declines without losing access to the store.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Prepare test contacts for a new subscriber, an existing email subscriber without SMS permission, an opted-out number, and an existing SMS subscriber.</li>
      <li>Inspect which audience each joins and whether signup starts a duplicate welcome journey.</li>
      <li>Change a preference or complete a test purchase while a message is queued. Observe whether the audience and queue update as your workflow requires.</li>
      <li>Test a reply needing support and a request to stop messages. Identify who sees each event and who handles exceptions.</li>
      <li>Repeat the storefront path on your important mobile devices and check that capture does not obstruct checkout.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Save the observed behavior and event timing. Do not treat this script as a passed test or extrapolate list growth from a vendor demonstration. If capture is the reason to move, compare signup completion and downstream permission quality in a properly scoped live pilot before replacing all existing forms.</p>
    <h2>Put service ownership in writing</h2>
    <p>A support promise is useful only when it fits your calendar. Ask who builds an audience, checks inventory and landing pages, approves an offer, schedules a send, and can cancel it. Include the hours during which your own team launches campaigns and the escalation path for a mistaken send. Do not assume support includes outsourced execution.</p>
    <p>For multiple stores, request a demonstration using your actual store and team structure. Check access boundaries, approval steps, and reporting exports before assuming that a larger contract solves governance. Store revenue alone does not establish the need for a particular platform.</p>
    <h2>Compare the work, not the enterprise label</h2>
    <p><a href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a> is an adjacent candidate when Shopify campaign execution is the immediate need. Compare the same capture, purchase-exit, and reply scenario; obtain a service proposal from each if you need operational help. The <a href="/compare/postscript-vs-attentive">pairwise comparison</a> turns those questions into a buying record.</p>
    <p><a href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a> deserves a separate look if the team already maintains its email audiences and flows there. Consolidating channels may reduce handoffs, but you still need to demonstrate the combined calendar and exclusions. Keeping an existing email provider can also be reasonable; verify the exact data exchange before assuming a pairing works.</p>
    <h2>Quote a normal month and a launch month</h2>
    <p>Give Attentive subscriber counts, destination mix, campaigns, automated traffic, message formats, and required services. Ask how each changes the price and what happens when a launch exceeds forecast. Request separate lines for the chosen channels and optional tools, plus minimums, renewal, cancellation, onboarding, and export terms.</p>
    <p>The Shopify label “free to install” is not a forecast of an operating bill. Use the <a href="/guides/pricing">pricing worksheet</a> to compare the written proposal against your current costs, including migration effort. A cheaper message rate is not enough if new service or channel commitments outweigh the saving.</p>
    <h2>Move permissions and exclusions before campaigns</h2>
    <p>Retain the source records for opt-ins and opt-outs, map channel-specific status, and reconcile import counts. An email subscriber is not automatically an SMS subscriber. Agree on the originating system for each property, including preferences and purchase events, and define how failures are surfaced.</p>
    <p>Pause overlapping flows during cutover and retain a rollback plan. Before broad sending, inspect one signup, one opt-out, one purchase exit, and one delayed event across the systems you will keep. Confirm destination restrictions and sending policies for your audience with the provider; a generic time window is not a complete policy.</p>
    <h2>Decision record</h2>
    <p>Choose only after you can name the acquisition or operating problem being solved, the observed trial behavior, included service responsibilities, full peak-month cost, and migration owner. Preserve campaign restraint by reviewing combined email and SMS exposure and excluding audiences whose current purchase or support situation makes the offer inappropriate. See <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination</a> for the calendar work that remains with the operator.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
